    "body": "partial fix on issue/266-tap-minor-sweep · PR #271 (draft) · pinned by `a_tap_in_a_hole_modelled_at_tap_drill_size_is_not_a_gouge` (+ guard `a_tap_wider_than_its_hole_is_still_a_gouge`) · `Refs`, not `Fixes`\n\nWhat it does: the program sim now sweeps a Tap-kind tool at its thread's core, `d/2 - 0.5413 x pitch` (ISO 724, the constant `thread_mill` already uses). On a plate with a Ø5 blind hole under `TAP-M6`, the post gate's sim clause went from a 12 mm false gouge to clean. A Ø3 hole is still a gouge.\n\n**Finding: a second check still refuses the tap.** Driving the branch build through `camcraft-cli mcp` (round Ø5 hole, `TAP-M6`, Drill with `cycle: tap`), `verify` now reports the program gate `ok`, but the op itself carries two blocking warnings: `move 1 cuts past the finish surface` and `move 2 cuts past the finish surface` (the feed in and out). They come from the generator's verify (`operation/generate.rs`), which hands `tool.geom()`, the full Ø6, to every law. Shrinking only the containment solid law's cutter (`contain_into`) did not clear them, because the tangency law flags the same moves.\n\nA fix there needs a decision, so I did not make one:\n- Use the core radius only for the cutting laws (tangency, solid), and keep the full diameter for the rapid laws. A tap must never rapid into the thread band, so the full diameter is the right rapid envelope. This means a second radius in the verifier's inputs (`VerifyContext`).\n- It also overturns the convention in `crates/app/tests/solid_law_goldens.rs` (\"the finished part's thread stands at the tap's own diameter\"), which models a tapped hole at the major diameter.\n\nSide note, not reproduced on `main` on its own: while testing I also ran a thread mill (Tap kind, Ø6 x 1) on a model with a ~Ø8 minor hole, `source` = the model. Its generator verify flagged most of its helix as `cuts past the finish surface`, plus `feed moves N and M cross in XY at shared depth` for the backward retract. So thread milling against a modelled hole may have the same problem and more. Existing thread-mill tests use `SrcRef::Stock`, so they don't exercise this.\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
